<p>08 · Bedroom Design</p>
<h2>The Deco bedroom is a private theatre — treat it like one</h2>
<p>Bedrooms are where Deco Revival delivers its most cinematic results. Homeowners are more willing to commit to bold colour behind a headboard than in a living room. Sleep is private, so the bedroom can be more emotional and less negotiated than public spaces. The design principles are:</p>
<h3>The hero move — a floor-to-ceiling upholstered headboard</h3>
<p>The single defining Deco bedroom feature is the floor-to-ceiling upholstered headboard wall. Channel-tufted in emerald velvet, scallop-tufted in blush, or fluted panelled in walnut with contrasting piping. Height is critical — 9 feet minimum, 10 feet ideal. Width should extend beyond the bed by 30–45 cm on each side. This one element carries 60% of the room's aesthetic.</p>
<h3>Bed and platform</h3>
<p>Low platform bed with 15–20 cm plinth base in walnut veneer or lacquer. Mattress height should be 45–50 cm total including plinth. Never a divan-with-storage in Deco — the storage goes into the wardrobe. King-size beds (72"×78" or 78"×84") are the default.</p>
<h3>Wardrobe design</h3>
<p>Sliding wardrobes with fluted or ribbed door fronts, or hinged wardrobes with panel-detail doors and brass long-pulls. If ceiling height permits, floor-to-ceiling always. Interior organisers with brass fittings and glass drawer fronts are the mid-to-premium upgrade. Standard laminate finish wardrobes work at entry tier if the fluted profile is retained.</p>
<h3>Signature bedroom elements</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pair of wall-mounted brass sconces</strong> flanking the bed at 140 cm from floor, replacing table lamps and freeing the nightstand.</li>
  <li><strong>Bench at the foot of the bed</strong> — 100–120 cm wide, velvet upholstered, on brass tapered legs.</li>
  <li><strong>Full-length arched mirror</strong> in a slim brass frame, leaning against the wall or wall-mounted.</li>
  <li><strong>Ceiling fixture</strong> — a small sunburst chandelier or fluted glass tiered pendant, centred over the foot of the bed rather than dead centre of the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Reading corner</strong> — single tub chair in blush velvet with brass arc floor lamp overhead, small marble side table.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Colour advice for bedrooms</span>Emerald in living rooms feels sophisticated. Emerald in bedrooms feels claustrophobic. Reserve the deep saturated hero for one accent wall (usually the headboard wall) and keep the remaining three walls in warm ivory or blush. Ceilings always in warm off-white — never coloured.</div>
<p>09 · Living Room Design</p>
<h2>Where entertaining lives — and where Deco earns its cost</h2>
<p>The Deco Revival living room is a public room designed for company. If your household does not entertain, does not have guests over for evening drinks or dinner, or would rather spend evenings alone with the television — Deco is the wrong style. The layout, materials, and money assume the room will be used.</p>
<h3>The anchor arrangement</h3>
<p>A single hero sofa (3-seater, deep jewel-tone velvet, curved back), flanked by two matching accent chairs (barrel or tub style, ivory or blush) forming a conversation triangle around a round marble coffee table. This arrangement seats 5–6 comfortably and reads as a room designed for people, not for screens.</p>
<h3>The feature wall</h3>
<p>Every Deco Revival living room needs one dominant feature wall. The options in order of impact are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fluted panelled feature wall</strong> — floor-to-ceiling walnut or macassar veneer flutes behind the sofa or the TV console. Highest visual impact, moderate cost (₹850–₹1,400 per sq ft).</li>
  <li><strong>Antique mirror panel wall</strong> — smoked or aged mirror in geometric segments, reflecting light and expanding the room. Highest cost, ideal for compact rooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Marble slab feature wall</strong> — book-matched Statuario or Nero Marquina, dramatic and permanent. Reserved for luxury tier.</li>
  <li><strong>Deep lacquered feature wall</strong> — emerald or sapphire high-gloss lacquer, hand-finished. Effective at low-to-mid cost but requires expert application.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The bar</h3>
<p>A dedicated bar area is almost mandatory in a full Deco Revival home. Options:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Freestanding bar cabinet</strong> — 90 cm wide, 180 cm tall, fluted front, mirrored interior with brass shelves and integrated LED.</li>
  <li><strong>Built-in bar niche</strong> — carved from the wall, floor-to-ceiling, mirrored back panel, marble shelf, brass hanging rack for glasses.</li>
  <li><strong>Bar corner with wet counter</strong> — 4-foot wide corner with marble counter, mini fridge, backlit bottle display, bar stools facing the room.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Lighting layers in the living room</h3>
<p>Five distinct lighting sources minimum: central statement chandelier or tiered pendant, floor lamp beside a reading chair, table lamps on side tables, wall sconces flanking the feature wall, and peripheral cove or picture lights. Every source dimmable, every temperature warm (2700K).</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The living room test</span>If you can imagine hosting six friends for cocktails in the room, the design is working. If the room is oriented purely toward a large-screen TV with all seating facing one direction, the design has failed the style. Deco living rooms accommodate TVs but never worship them.</div>
<p>10 · Dining Room Design</p>
<h2>Where Deco is most itself</h2>
<p>If forced to pick one room where Deco Revival delivers the highest return per rupee, it would be the dining room. Diners are seated for extended periods with nowhere to look but at the room itself. Every detail matters more here than anywhere else.</p>
<h3>The table</h3>
<p>Round pedestal tables seat 4–6 in warmth; oval tables seat 8–10 with formality. Marble tops are the default — Statuario, Nero Marquina, or Green Guatemala depending on the palette. Bases are the hero — fluted brass, sculpted lacquered wood, or an architectural pedestal in the hero material. Reserve at least ₹1.2–₹2.5 lakh for a proper Deco dining table; the flat MDF-and-veneer versions available at general retailers cannot carry the style.</p>
<h3>The chairs</h3>
<p>Upholstered dining chairs in performance velvet — channel-tufted backs, scallop-shaped backs, or clean rolled-back barrel silhouettes. Six or eight matching, or pairs of end chairs contrasting with side chairs. Seat height 46–48 cm, seat depth 45–50 cm. Fabric selection is critical — real velvets from D'Decor's performance range, not the polyester-blend velvets sold at general retailers, which pill within two years.</p>
<h3>The pendant</h3>
<p>The dining pendant is a hero object. Options:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Tiered brass chandelier</strong> — three or five-tier construction with fluted glass or crystal detailing.</li>
  <li><strong>Sunburst chandelier</strong> — radiating brass arms, small bulb clusters, statement scale.</li>
  <li><strong>Linear brass fixture</strong> — for long oval tables, matching the table's proportions.</li>
  <li><strong>Cluster of three matched pendants</strong> — the safe modern move for open-plan homes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pendant should hang 75–85 cm above the table surface. Diameter should be 60–70% of the table width for round tables, 50% of the length for oval tables.</p>
<h3>The sideboard</h3>
<p>Every Deco dining room needs a sideboard or credenza. 180 cm minimum length, 45 cm depth, waist-height. Walnut or macassar veneer with brass long-pulls, or lacquered with fluted detail. Function is real — serves as buffet during dinners, holds tableware, and displays sculpture or a table lamp.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The dining-room test</span>If the room feels equally good with candles lit and the pendant dimmed as it does with full lighting, the palette and materials are right. If it needs full lighting to look complete, the underlying design is missing something.</div>
<p>11 · Bathroom Design</p>
<h2>The one room where restraint is punished</h2>
<p>Bathrooms in Deco Revival homes are permitted — even encouraged — to be more dramatic than any other room. This is the room where marble slab installations, tiered brass chandeliers, and octagonal floor patterns actually make sense. The 1930s Deco masters treated bathrooms as private spas, and the revival honours that.</p>
<h3>The vanity</h3>
<p>Floating vanity, 120–160 cm wide, fluted or ribbed front in walnut veneer or lacquered finish. Marble slab counter (Statuario or Calacatta) with either a single undermount basin or two vessel basins for master bathrooms. Brushed brass tapware — wall-mounted for the cleanest lines, or deck-mounted crossed-lever taps for a more historical detail.</p>
<h3>Flooring</h3>
<p>Deco floors are where geometry gets its clearest showcase. Options:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Octagonal marble mosaic</strong> — Nero Marquina octagons with white marble insets, or Statuario octagons with black marble insets.</li>
  <li><strong>Chevron patterned marble</strong> — Statuario chevrons for a lighter feel, or contrasting marble chevrons for drama.</li>
  <li><strong>Herringbone marble</strong> — traditional and always right.</li>
  <li><strong>Feature-tile inlay</strong> — a central medallion or border pattern in encaustic or handmade tile, surrounded by plain marble.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Walls</h3>
<p>Floor-to-ceiling marble slab on the wet wall behind the vanity — book-matched for high impact. The remaining walls in flat lime plaster or venetian plaster in warm ivory. Never floor-to-ceiling small tile in Deco Revival — the grout lines destroy the marble aesthetic.</p>
<h3>Fixtures and mirror</h3>
<p>Arched mirrors, round mirrors, or scalloped mirrors in slim brass frames. Never rectangular framed mirrors above vanities — they look bank-like. Brass wall sconces flanking the mirror at eye level. Ceiling pendant if height permits — even a small tiered brass chandelier above a freestanding tub is legitimate Deco.</p>
<h3>The bathtub question</h3>
<p>Freestanding tubs — clawfoot or pedestal — are Deco Revival's ideal. Practical in villas with generous master bathrooms, difficult in Bangalore apartments where bathroom floor plates are under 60 sq ft. If space permits, a freestanding tub becomes the room's hero. If not, skip it entirely rather than compromising with a built-in tub.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Compact bathroom Deco tip</span>In a 40–50 sq ft compact bathroom, Deco Revival still works. The formula: black octagonal floor tile with white insets, single slab of Statuario marble on the wet wall, walnut veneer vanity with brass hardware, single arched brass mirror, one brass wall sconce. Total additional cost over a standard bathroom: ₹85,000–₹1,20,000.</div>
<p>12 · Lighting Blueprint</p>
<h2>Get the light wrong and nothing else matters</h2>
<p>Deco Revival is more sensitive to lighting than any other contemporary style. The materials — brass, marble, velvet, lacquer — all react to light temperature and beam angle. A perfect Deco room lit with cool 4000K downlights looks like a hotel lobby at 2 am. The same room at 2700K with layered warm sources looks like a film set.</p>
<h3>The five-layer rule</h3>
<p>Every primary room needs all five layers:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Ambient</strong> — cove lighting, ceiling wash, general fill. Warm white (2700K), dimmable. Never the primary light source in a Deco room.</li>
  <li><strong>Task</strong> — reading lamps, kitchen under-cabinet, vanity lights. Warm white (2700–3000K).</li>
  <li><strong>Accent</strong> — spotlights on art, uplights on plants, wash on textured feature walls. Warm white, narrow beam.</li>
  <li><strong>Decorative</strong> — chandeliers, sconces, table lamps. These are furniture as much as lighting.</li>
  <li><strong>Peripheral</strong> — cove behind curtains, LED strips under floating vanities, backlit fluted glass. Adds depth and drama.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Signature Deco fixtures</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sunburst chandelier</strong> — radiating brass arms, ideal for foyers and dining rooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Tiered brass pendant</strong> — three or five graduated tiers, sometimes with fluted glass shades. Foyer or dining room.</li>
  <li><strong>Fluted glass pendant</strong> — single opal or ribbed glass shade on brass stem. Kitchen island or breakfast counter.</li>
  <li><strong>Sconces with fluted glass shades</strong> — wall-mounted, in matching pairs. Flanking mirrors, headboards, or art.</li>
  <li><strong>Arc floor lamp</strong> with brass base and marble weight, curved arm reaching over a reading chair.</li>
  <li><strong>Domed table lamps</strong> in lacquered wood or ceramic bases with brass detail.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Colour temperature discipline</h3>
<p>Every fixture in the home should be 2700K. Task lights in the kitchen may go to 3000K but not warmer than that. Never 4000K, never 5000K, never "daylight" bulbs. The consistency is what allows the eye to relax and the materials to sing.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><span class="callout__title">Common lighting failure</span>Homeowners buy the correct decorative fixtures but retain the builder-provided 4000K downlights across the ceiling. Result: every marble surface reads grey, every brass reads flat, every velvet reads dusty. Fix: replace every downlight bulb with 2700K dimmable, budget approximately ₹8,000–₹15,000 for the whole home. Highest-return low-cost intervention available.</div>
<p>13 · Decor, Art &amp; Objects</p>
<h2>The layer that turns a designed room into a lived-in home</h2>
<p>Decor and art are where most Deco Revival projects lose their way. Homeowners spend heavily on cabinetry and furniture, then run out of budget and taste when it comes to the accessorising layer. The result is a room that feels 70% complete. Here is what belongs — and what to avoid.</p>
<h3>Art</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Original prints or large-scale posters</strong> from the 1925–1935 travel-poster era (Air France, Orient Express, Normandie ocean liner) — reproductions from AllPosters or vintage-print specialists work fine.</li>
  <li><strong>Geometric abstract paintings</strong> in the Cassandre or Tamara de Lempicka tradition — commissioned from Indian artists at ₹15,000–₹80,000 per piece.</li>
  <li><strong>Black-and-white photography</strong> in slim brass frames — architectural, portrait, or fashion editorial from the era.</li>
  <li><strong>Sculptural pieces</strong> — a single small brass or bronze sculpture on a console or credenza is worth more visually than a dozen small objects.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mirrors</h3>
<p>Mirrors are structural in Deco. Arched sunburst mirrors over consoles, round brass-frame mirrors above vanities, floor-to-ceiling mirror panels on feature walls, antique-mirror ceiling detail in dining rooms. Every home should have at least three deliberate mirror placements.</p>
<h3>Textiles</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Silk-blend cushions in ivory, blush, and jewel tones — mix solid velvet with subtle geometric jacquard.</li>
  <li>Linen or silk-blend drapery — floor-to-ceiling, pinch-pleat headings, no valances. Sheer under, heavy over.</li>
  <li>Wool or silk-blend rugs — geometric Deco patterns, or plain in warm neutrals. Sized generously — a rug that only sits under the coffee table is a decorating error.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Objects</h3>
<p>Deco decor lives on horizontal surfaces — consoles, coffee tables, sideboards, credenza tops. Group in odd numbers (3 or 5), vary heights, mix material (brass sculpture + ceramic vessel + hardcover book stack). Every horizontal surface should have a moment. Every moment should be intentional.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Editing rule</span>The first pass of accessorising in a Deco home always overdoes it. Once you've placed everything, remove one-third. The room will improve. If the room feels too spare after the removal, add back only one piece.</div>
<p>14 · Which Home Types Suit It</p>
<h2>Not every apartment can carry Deco. Here's the honest answer.</h2>
<p>Style suitability is a real thing. Some floor plans, ceiling heights, and window configurations are structurally sympathetic to Deco Revival. Others fight it. Here is the honest matrix.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Home type</th><th>Suitability</th><th>Why</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Villas / independent houses (2000+ sqft)</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>Generous rooms, higher ceilings, multiple public spaces — ideal Deco canvas</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Penthouse apartments</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>High ceilings, large windows, room for feature walls and statement lighting</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Premium 3BHK / 4BHK apartments (1600+ sqft)</td><td>■■■■□</td><td>Works beautifully if ceilings are 10 feet or higher; standard 9-foot ceilings need careful editing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standard 3BHK apartments (1200–1500 sqft)</td><td>■■■□□</td><td>Requires editing — smaller-scale furniture, restrained colour palette, no full-height headboards</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compact 2BHK apartments (750–1000 sqft)</td><td>■■□□□</td><td>Possible but restrictive — reserve the style to one room (bedroom or living), rest of home in neutral warm minimalism</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Studio apartments (under 600 sqft)</td><td>■□□□□</td><td>Not recommended as full-home style — the visual density overwhelms compact spaces</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Heritage bungalows with original architecture</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>Deco was born for architectural interiors — original mouldings, high ceilings, and traditional window proportions are perfect</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Non-negotiable requirements</span>Minimum 9-foot ceilings, minimum 200 sq ft living room, ability to install layered lighting (which means false ceiling or cove-friendly ceiling), and windows that admit natural light. If your home fails any of these tests, consider Modern Classic or Warm Contemporary as more forgiving alternatives.</div>
<p>15 · Ideal Room Sizes</p>
<h2>What each room needs to breathe</h2>
<p>Deco furniture is heavier than modern furniture. Deco lighting hangs lower and takes visual space. Feature walls need width to read. These are the minimum room sizes for the style to work well:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Room</th><th>Minimum</th><th>Ideal</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Living room</td><td>200 sq ft</td><td>280+ sq ft</td><td>Needs feature wall of at least 12 feet width</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dining room</td><td>140 sq ft</td><td>180+ sq ft</td><td>Round 6-seater needs 12x12 minimum</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Master bedroom</td><td>140 sq ft</td><td>180+ sq ft</td><td>Full-height headboard needs 12+ foot ceiling width</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kitchen (island)</td><td>140 sq ft</td><td>180+ sq ft</td><td>Island requires 90 cm clearance all sides</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Master bathroom</td><td>60 sq ft</td><td>90+ sq ft</td><td>Freestanding tub needs 100+ sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Foyer</td><td>25 sq ft</td><td>45+ sq ft</td><td>Chandelier + console + arched mirror needs presence</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Below these minimums, the style requires substantial adaptation — scale down statement pieces, drop full-height feature walls in favour of framed mirror panels, use one hero fixture per room instead of layered lighting.</p>
<p>16 · Who Is This Style For</p>
<h2>Four buyer profiles this style genuinely fits</h2>
<h4>The entertainer</h4>
<p>Hosts monthly dinners, drinks-and-conversation evenings, weekend brunches. Wants their home to be the venue their circle actually looks forward to. Deco Revival is designed around exactly this pattern of use.</p>
<h4>The style commitment couple</h4>
<p>Late 30s to mid 40s, second home or major upgrade, done with rental furniture, interested in something with historical depth. Willing to invest ₹18–₹35 lakh in interiors and expect the design to last 15+ years without dating.</p>
<h4>The hospitality-adjacent</h4>
<p>Hoteliers, restaurateurs, boutique brand owners, film industry professionals, senior creative-industry executives — buyers whose professional life is soaked in curated aesthetics and who expect their home to match.</p>
<h4>The heritage-property owner</h4>
<p>Owns or inherited a pre-Independence bungalow, an older builder floor, or a heritage-listed property with high ceilings and original mouldings. Deco Revival is the only contemporary style that respects and extends this architecture.</p>
<h3>Buyers this style does not fit</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Households with young children under 6 — the marble surfaces, velvet upholstery, and delicate brass accessories are practical mismatches for the sticky-hands phase of life.</li>
  <li>Buyers whose evenings are spent alone with the television — the style is designed for social use.</li>
  <li>Homeowners with tight compact floor plans and standard 9-foot ceilings who want the full style throughout the home.</li>
  <li>Those who dislike upkeep — Deco materials reward maintenance and punish neglect.</li>
</ul>
<p>17 · Advantages</p>
<h2>Why homeowners pick this style over safer alternatives</h2>
<h3>1. It ages well because it's already old</h3>
<p>Every style that is not currently trendy will one day become the current trend or the current out-of-fashion. Deco has been through both cycles multiple times. Nothing in a well-executed Deco Revival home will look dated in 2036, because it already looked "dated" in 2016 and 1996 and 1976. Timelessness by rejection of trend.</p>
<h3>2. Materials appreciate rather than depreciate</h3>
<p>Real marble, brass, hardwood veneers, and quality upholstery age into their finish rather than out of it. The patina on brushed brass, the wear on marble edges, the softening of velvet — all improve the home visually over years.</p>
<h3>3. Resale value is strong</h3>
<p>Homes with distinctive design command higher prices than homes with generic interior packages, particularly in the ₹1.5 crore-plus segment where buyers value differentiation. Deco Revival is a documented resale-positive style.</p>
<h3>4. Works across scale</h3>
<p>The style adapts from 1200 sq ft apartments to 5000 sq ft villas with appropriate editing. The visual language is scalable — which cannot be said of Hollywood Regency, Maximalism, or several other high-drama styles.</p>
<h3>5. Genuinely photographable</h3>
<p>Homeowners increasingly want homes that photograph well for personal social media and eventual sale listings. Deco's geometric symmetry, contrast, and considered material palette photographs better than any contemporary style.</p>
<h3>6. Delivers hospitality quality at home</h3>
<p>Guests notice the difference immediately. The overwhelming feedback we receive from Deco Revival homeowners is that guests behave differently in the space — dressed more thoughtfully, stayed longer, spoke slower.</p>
<p>18 · Disadvantages</p>
<h2>Honest downsides most designers gloss over</h2>
<h3>1. Higher cost floor</h3>
<p>The materials are more expensive. Real veneer over laminate. Real marble over laminated stone. Real brass over painted hardware. Real velvet over polyester blend. There is no ₹1,200 per sq ft version of this style — the minimum floor is around ₹1,800 per sq ft, and premium execution starts at ₹2,800.</p>
<h3>2. Requires design competence</h3>
<p>The style is unforgiving. A generic contractor cannot execute it. You need a designer who understands proportion, motif, and material hierarchy — which limits your service provider options.</p>
<h3>3. Higher maintenance</h3>
<p>Marble stains, brass tarnishes if uncoated, velvet needs vacuuming, mirrors need cleaning. The maintenance is not brutal but it is real — this is not a "install once, forget for a decade" style like laminate-and-fabric contemporary.</p>
<h3>4. Can feel formal for daily life</h3>
<p>Some households enter a Deco home and never quite relax in it. The style has ceremony baked in. If your family life is casual and pyjamas-forward, the disconnect between design and daily behaviour will show.</p>
<h3>5. Small-space limitations</h3>
<p>Below 900 sq ft carpet area, executing full Deco Revival becomes an exercise in constant editing. Homeowners often end up with a home that looks 80% right and 20% cramped, which is worse than picking a more forgiving style.</p>
<h3>6. Photographs harder than it lives</h3>
<p>The counter-point to advantage #5 — some Deco Revival homes photograph brilliantly but feel less inviting at ground level than the images suggest. Good designers correct for this by softening with textiles, plants, and casual layering. Bad designers deliver a home that only works for its own listing photos.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Balanced view</span>Every advantage of Deco Revival comes with a corresponding cost — in money, in maintenance, in behaviour, or in floor plan requirements. Homeowners who understand these upfront are consistently thrilled with the outcome. Homeowners who see only the aesthetic and skip the trade-offs are consistently disappointed within 18 months.</div>
