<h3>Where homeowners should overspend</h3>
<p>Bar cabinet (or built-in bar), dining chandelier, master bedroom headboard, and one hero marble surface. These four items carry disproportionate visual weight.</p>
<h3>Where homeowners should underspend</h3>
<p>Guest bedroom furniture, utility area cabinetry, and secondary bathroom finishes. These rooms are seen less often and can use one-tier-lower material specifications without visual penalty.</p>
<p>23 · Compared With Other Styles</p>
<h2>How Art Deco Revival differs from the styles it gets confused with</h2>
<p>Deco Revival sits in a neighbourhood of related styles — some are close cousins, some are opposites wearing similar clothing. This is the field guide.</p>
<h3>Deco Revival vs Hollywood Regency</h3>
<p>Hollywood Regency is Deco's louder American cousin. Both use velvet, brass, and geometric patterns. Regency turns up the theatrical volume — brighter jewel tones, more animal print, more mirror, more Lucite. Deco Revival is quieter, more European, more architectural. If Regency is Beverly Hills 1978, Deco Revival is Paris 1932. Choose Deco Revival for restraint, Regency for pure glamour.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Deco Revival</th><th>Hollywood Regency</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Colour saturation</td><td>Medium-high, jewel tones with warmth</td><td>High, brighter and cleaner</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pattern density</td><td>Restrained, geometric</td><td>Higher, animal print welcome</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Metal finish</td><td>Brushed brass, warm</td><td>Bright brass and gold, sometimes chrome</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Furniture silhouette</td><td>Sculptural, architectural</td><td>Curvier, more theatrical</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Sophisticated urban homes</td><td>Statement personalities, entertainers</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Deco Revival vs Modern Classic</h3>
<p>Modern Classic is the current mainstream premium Indian style — beige and cream palette, moulded panelling, upholstered headboards, chandeliers. It shares Deco's fondness for hospitality-inspired materials but lacks the geometry and colour discipline. Modern Classic is safer, more universal, easier to resell to any buyer profile. Deco Revival is more distinctive and demands more from the homeowner.</p>
<h3>Deco Revival vs Contemporary Luxury</h3>
<p>Contemporary Luxury (as seen in most premium apartment showrooms) uses similar materials — marble, brass, veneer, velvet — but strips out the historical vocabulary. No sunbursts, no chevrons, no fluting, no arches. The result is expensive-looking but flatter. Deco Revival adds the storytelling layer that Contemporary Luxury lacks.</p>
<h3>Deco Revival vs Maximalism</h3>
<p>Both styles reject minimalism. Maximalism piles on colour, pattern, and objects with almost no rules. Deco Revival is disciplined maximalism — bold, layered, and material-rich, but held together by strict geometric grammar. Maximalism is easier to execute badly, harder to execute well; Deco Revival is harder to execute badly because it has more rules.</p>
<h3>Deco Revival vs Mid-Century Modern</h3>
<p>Both are historical revivals of 20th-century movements. MCM is warmer, lighter, more casual, less decorated. Deco is heavier, darker, more formal, more decorated. MCM works better for casual family living. Deco works better for entertaining-forward households. Both age well.</p>
<h3>Deco Revival vs Traditional Indian</h3>
<p>Traditional Indian uses hand-carved wood, jaali screens, brass with warm colour, and cultural motifs. Interestingly, it can be blended beautifully with Deco Revival — both love brass, both love geometry, both love craft. Indo-Deco is a documented emerging sub-style, particularly effective in South Indian villas and Rajasthani heritage properties.</p>
