<p>24 · Common Execution Mistakes</p>
<h2>The traps we see repeatedly in Bangalore homes</h2>
<h3>Mistake 1 — Bright polished chrome/gold hardware</h3>
<p>Homeowners buy the cheapest hardware from the local supplier assuming metal is metal. Bright polished chrome-plated brass looks plastic under LEDs and destroys the aesthetic instantly. Always specify brushed brass or antique brass with PVD coating.</p>
<h3>Mistake 2 — Fluting everywhere</h3>
<p>Fluting is Deco's most-abused motif. Homeowners flute the kitchen island, wardrobe fronts, headboard, TV panel, dining base, and bar cabinet — all simultaneously. The result reads as decorative panic. Rule: two fluted surfaces per room maximum. One is often enough.</p>
<h3>Mistake 3 — Underscaled lighting</h3>
<p>A 40 cm pendant over an 8-seater dining table looks like a bike light. Deco lighting is generous — dining pendants should be 65–80 cm diameter for a 6-seater and 90–110 cm for an 8-seater.</p>
<h3>Mistake 4 — All jewel tones, no ivory</h3>
<p>Emerald sofa, sapphire chairs, ruby drapes, black cabinets. Without the ivory/warm neutral base carrying 55–60% of the room, the jewel tones fight each other and the room becomes exhausting.</p>
<h3>Mistake 5 — Cheap velvet</h3>
<p>Polyester "velvet" from generic upholsterers pills within 18 months, sheds fibres, and looks synthetic in direct light. Only performance velvet from D'Decor, Sarom, or comparable brands survives long-term use.</p>
<h3>Mistake 6 — Wrong ceiling colour</h3>
<p>Homeowners paint ceilings in the same warm ivory as the walls, which is correct. Then they extend the wall accent colour to the ceiling, which is not. Deco ceilings stay warm off-white. Coloured ceilings compress the room's height.</p>
<h3>Mistake 7 — Faux marble laminate</h3>
<p>Laminated stone patterns as kitchen backsplash or dining tables read as fake within 3 feet. Either use real marble/quartz or use a completely different material — never fake marble. The compromise fools no one.</p>
<h3>Mistake 8 — Ignoring the ceiling</h3>
<p>Deco was born from ceiling detail — coffered ceilings, cove-lit steps, ceiling roses, and painted geometry. Modern ceilings are often flat gyp-board with recessed downlights. At least add coves, at minimum a ceiling rose around the central chandelier.</p>
<h3>Mistake 9 — Overusing mirror</h3>
<p>Mirror is powerful in Deco but self-cancelling if overused. Two mirrored feature walls, mirrored cabinets, mirrored splashbacks, and mirrored consoles — all in the same visual field — creates a funhouse. Rule: one dominant mirror moment per room.</p>
<h3>Mistake 10 — Buying "Deco" from generic retailers</h3>
<p>Mass-market retailers sell "Art Deco style" furniture that is generic Chinese contemporary with a token gold accent. Almost none of it works. Legitimate Deco Revival furniture requires custom fabrication or purchases from specialists — either factory-direct workshops or curated design retailers.</p>
<p>25 · Designer Field Tips</p>
<h2>Twenty things we've learnt from installing Deco Revival homes</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Commission one hero piece.</strong> A custom bar cabinet, an oversized headboard, a monumental console. One piece the home is designed around.</li>
  <li><strong>Match wood tones across the home.</strong> Pick one veneer (walnut or macassar) and use it as the connecting thread through kitchen, TV wall, dining sideboard, and bedroom.</li>
  <li><strong>Ivory is not white.</strong> Specify wall paint as warm off-white with a slight yellow undertone, never cool white. Test three shades before finalising.</li>
  <li><strong>Curved sofa, not straight.</strong> A curved-back sofa is worth ₹40,000 more than a straight-back — one detail carries the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Overspend on the dining chandelier.</strong> Every rupee spent here returns 3x visually.</li>
  <li><strong>Layer three light temperatures? Never.</strong> All lighting at 2700K. Consistency is discipline.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy the rug last.</strong> After all upholstery is delivered, the rug becomes obvious. Buying it first almost always leads to replacement.</li>
  <li><strong>Ceiling coves change the room.</strong> Adding ambient cove lighting behind existing false-ceiling steps adds architectural depth for under ₹15,000 per room.</li>
  <li><strong>Full-length drapery, always.</strong> Curtains that stop above the floor are decorating errors. Puddle or kiss the floor by 1–2 cm.</li>
  <li><strong>Handleless is not Deco.</strong> Recess-groove cabinets kill the aesthetic. Deco needs long brass pulls or brass knobs — hardware is jewellery.</li>
  <li><strong>Symmetry before individuality.</strong> Twin chairs, twin sconces, twin lamps, twin planters. Individuality is added by objects, not by asymmetry.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy real art, not prints from generic decor retailers.</strong> One good piece is worth ten average ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Antique the mirror.</strong> New mirrors are too clean. Silvering flaws on antique glass add age and depth. Custom fabricated at ₹550–₹800 per sqft.</li>
  <li><strong>Skip the pooja room debate.</strong> Deco Revival can accommodate a pooja room beautifully — brass, marble, and warm lighting are already the vocabulary. Design it in a niche with a fluted wooden frame.</li>
  <li><strong>Use one bold wallpaper.</strong> Foyer, powder room, or dining alcove. A single wall of geometric Deco wallpaper (from designers like Cole &amp; Son, House of Hackney) elevates the whole home.</li>
  <li><strong>Fresh flowers.</strong> The style expects them. Even a single bud vase on the dining sideboard changes the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy the pieces that photograph, not the pieces on discount.</strong> The bar cabinet, the dining chandelier, the master headboard. These are the moments guests remember.</li>
  <li><strong>Add books.</strong> Deco was born in a literary age. A dining sideboard styled with a small stack of hardcover books tells the truth about the household.</li>
  <li><strong>The bathroom deserves the chandelier.</strong> A small brass chandelier over a master bathroom vanity turns utility into ritual. Under ₹18,000 in most cases.</li>
  <li><strong>Live in the home before adding the last 20% of decor.</strong> After 6 weeks, gaps and needs become obvious. The decor bought at that point is always better than the decor bought at handover.</li>
</ol>
