<p>30 · Closing Thoughts</p>
<h2>Why we recommend this style — and to whom</h2>
<p>Art Deco Revival is one of a small number of interior design styles that respects both the eye and the hand. It rewards good sourcing, punishes shortcuts, and returns the investment across decades rather than years. In a decorating culture that increasingly optimises for photographable moments over lived experience, Deco is one of the styles that photographs beautifully <em>because</em> it lives beautifully.</p>
<p>We recommend it to households that entertain, appreciate materials, and are prepared to invest in a home meant to last twenty years rather than five. We recommend against it for casual family homes with toddlers, homes under 900 sq ft carpet area, and homeowners whose primary interest is optimising cost per square foot.</p>
<p>The Bangalore market for this style has matured meaningfully in the last three years. Skilled workshops, quality material suppliers, and experienced designers are all more accessible now than they were even in 2022. Costs have stabilised. Sourcing is faster. Execution risk is lower.</p>
<p>The right time to build an Art Deco Revival home is when you own the property, plan to stay in it for a decade or more, and want the daily experience of living somewhere considered. This guide has attempted to give you the language, the numbers, and the trade-offs to make that decision honestly.</p>
<p>If you'd like to talk through your specific home, floor plan, or budget with one of our designers — no obligation, no design fee at the discussion stage — we'd be glad to.</p>
<h2>Expert notes, checklists, and the truth about hidden costs</h2>
<p>Written by senior designers with 8+ years each in residential Indian interiors. These notes cover the questions clients most often ask us privately, after the main proposal has been signed.</p>
<h3>Interior designer notes</h3>
<p>Deco Revival is technically demanding to specify. The proportions, motif discipline, material hierarchy, and lighting all interact. A designer new to the style typically over-uses one element (usually fluting or brass) and under-uses another (usually ceiling detail or scale). Ask your designer to show you three completed Deco Revival projects before committing. If they cannot, they're learning on your money.</p>
<h3>Residential architect notes</h3>
<p>Structural changes to accommodate Deco often work in your favour — widening a doorway to a full arch, extending an internal wall to accommodate a bar niche, adding a pilaster to frame a bed. These are minor structural interventions in most apartments (₹35,000–₹85,000 including permissions) that transform the design potential.</p>
<h3>Material sourcing notes</h3>
<p>The three highest-risk materials in a Deco Revival home are marble (colour and vein variation between slabs), veneer (sheet variation and matching), and brass (finish consistency across suppliers). All three need physical inspection at the sourcing stage, not photographs. Insist on visiting the marble yard and the veneer warehouse yourself — one 4-hour Saturday spent doing this prevents ₹2–3 lakh of downstream problems.</p>
<h3>Human behaviour notes</h3>
<p>Homeowners live in Deco Revival homes differently than in generic contemporary homes. Dinner parties are held at home more often. Guests dress more thoughtfully. Household members change clothes when returning home rather than staying in office clothes. These behavioural shifts are consistent enough across our client base that they're worth mentioning — the home changes the way you live in it.</p>
<h3>Colour psychology notes</h3>
<p>Deep emerald triggers focus and calm — appropriate for living rooms and dining. Deep sapphire encourages rest — appropriate for bedrooms. Ruby encourages appetite and conversation — appropriate for dining rooms but exhausting in bedrooms. Blush is a soft, restful accent for feminine dressing spaces or master bedroom detail. Choosing the wrong jewel tone for a room produces subtle daily discomfort that homeowners notice but often cannot name.</p>
<h3>Maintenance reality notes</h3>
<p>The maintenance burden of Deco Revival is not the cleaning — it's the discipline of not letting the home slide. A perfectly-maintained Deco home is spectacular. A neglected Deco home looks worse than a neglected minimalist home because there's more to look at. If your household is not maintenance-disciplined, choose a more forgiving style.</p>
<h3>Cost notes</h3>
<p>The single largest cost surprise in Deco Revival homes is site-installed marble work — cutting, mitering, edge-polishing, sealing, and installing large slabs is skilled labour at ₹120–₹180 per sqft over and above the material cost. Budget this line separately; do not let contractors bury it into an all-in per-sqft figure.</p>
<h3>Customer buying checklist</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Have I confirmed my ceiling height allows this style</li>
  <li>Have I planned for at least ₹1,800/sqft interior budget</li>
  <li>Have I evaluated whether my household actually entertains</li>
  <li>Do I have 12–14 weeks available for execution</li>
  <li>Am I willing to maintain marble, brass, and velvet</li>
  <li>Have I seen my designer's completed Deco projects</li>
</ul>
<h3>Room planning checklist</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Every room has a hero material (marble, veneer, lacquer, or mirror)</li>
  <li>Every room has at least three lighting layers</li>
  <li>Fluting is limited to two surfaces maximum per room</li>
  <li>Symmetry is established before any deliberate asymmetric element</li>
  <li>Ceiling has some detail (cove, rose, moulding, or step)</li>
  <li>Ivory or warm neutral carries 55–60% of the visual field</li>
</ul>
<h3>Budget planning checklist</h3>
<ul>
  <li>18–22% allocated to modular kitchen</li>
  <li>14–18% allocated to wardrobes</li>
  <li>16–20% allocated to living and dining furniture</li>
  <li>8–10% allocated to lighting fixtures</li>
  <li>5–8% reserved for contingency</li>
  <li>3–5% budgeted for post-handover decor and art</li>
</ul>
<h3>Pre-execution checklist</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Physical inspection of marble slabs before purchase</li>
  <li>Veneer sheet numbers documented for book-matching</li>
  <li>Brass hardware confirmed as PVD-coated for humidity</li>
  <li>Lighting bulbs confirmed as 2700K dimmable</li>
  <li>Fabric samples tested for pill and stain resistance</li>
  <li>All curtain rails installed before drapery fabrication</li>
</ul>
<h3>Post-installation checklist</h3>
<ul>
  <li>All marble surfaces sealed with penetrating sealer</li>
  <li>All brass hardware wiped and verified for finish uniformity</li>
  <li>Lighting scenes programmed if dimmer-controller installed</li>
  <li>Snagging list completed and signed off</li>
  <li>Maintenance card provided to housekeeping staff</li>
  <li>Six-week decor review scheduled with designer</li>
</ul>
<h3>Hidden costs commonly missed</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Marble installation labour (₹120–₹180 per sqft)</li>
  <li>PVD coating on brass hardware (10–15% additional over uncoated)</li>
  <li>Cove lighting drivers and dimmer modules (₹8,000–₹15,000 per room)</li>
  <li>Curtain hardware — full brass rods, brackets, finials (₹4,500–₹8,000 per window)</li>
  <li>Skirting upgrades — brass inlay, tall skirtings, painted profiles (₹85–₹280 per running foot)</li>
  <li>Art commissioning (typical serious buyer spends ₹1.5–3 lakh on art post-handover)</li>
  <li>Fresh floral arrangements — an ongoing running cost of ₹3,000–₹8,000 per month if maintained</li>
</ul>
<h3>Common client misconceptions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>"Gold and brass are the same" — they are not. Bright gold plate ages badly; brushed brass ages beautifully.</li>
  <li>"Real marble is impossible to maintain" — it is not. Yearly sealing and immediate spill wipe-up is enough.</li>
  <li>"Deco needs all-black or all-dark rooms" — it does not. Ivory-based Deco is often more sophisticated.</li>
  <li>"I can save by using laminate marble prints" — you cannot. The compromise fools no one.</li>
  <li>"Chandeliers are only for grand homes" — a modest 60 cm chandelier looks beautiful in a standard dining room.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Professional warnings</h3>
<p>Beware: designers offering Deco Revival at rates below ₹1,600 per sqft. The materials cannot mathematically fit. Either the veneer will be inferior, the brass will be plated pot metal, or the "marble" will be laminate. Insist on line-item BOQs with brand and quality specifications, not lump-sum quotes.</p>
